{
  "image_name": "2f1e05a5273a2931774645f5bd6cba5e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a 2D perovskite dopant is incorporated at the boundaries of a 3D perovskite crystal and how this creates a mixed 2D/3D structure with altered electronic behavior. The top part shows 2D layers inserted between regions of 3D lattice. Colored atomic sites distinguish the main ions, and the diagrams suggest that bulky organic cations form thin 2D slabs at grain boundaries while the 3D framework remains on either side.\n\nThe accompanying band diagrams indicate why this matters electronically: the 2D regions have different conduction- and valence-band positions than the 3D material, creating barriers that appear to confine or redirect charge carriers. Electrons and holes are shown accumulating in the 3D domains with curved arrows indicating interfacial transfer or blocking effects at the 2D layers.\n\nThe lower left panel presents a device-like stack, where a mixed perovskite film contains a mottled upper region associated with 2D components distributed in a 3D matrix. The lower right compares crystal structures of a conventional tin iodide perovskite and a bulky-cation-derived layered phase, highlighting how the larger organic spacer disrupts the 3D connectivity and produces the layered 2D architecture.",
